    "split": "train",
    "coverage": "factual detail, attribution",
    "Original": "After I first started writing a column, in the fall of 2002, it wasn't long before I heard regularly from those who brandished God as a weapon in opposing LGBTQ rights. This was not surprising. Back then, I was on staff at The Plain Dealer in Cleveland, and I was long familiar with the bigotry in my home state. Most of the hate mail came from strangers, but I got my share of lectures from blood relatives, too. Few things anger right-wing Christians more than a family member insisting she's acquainted with a different version of God.",
    "v2_output": "After I began writing a column in the fall of 2002, it wasn't long before I received regular correspondence from individuals who used God as a tool to oppose LGBTQ rights. This was unsurprising. At the time, I was employed at The Plain Dealer in Cleveland, and I was already well-acquainted with the bigotry in my home state. Most of the hate mail came from anonymous sources, but I also received lectures from family members. Few things infuriate right-wing Christians more than a family member who claims to know a different version of God.",
